E-1 / T2.8 -- does row alignment earn its memory?
=================================================

The BENEFIT side of X-1, which measured only the cost. Gates D-4, the project's
only PROVISIONAL decision. NOT a binCV-versus-OpenCV comparison: it compares
binCV against binCV at four row alignments, so ARCHITECTURE 10.3's denominator
does not apply.

DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE MEASURING (TASKS.md T2.8, recorded in
EXPERIMENTS.md X-9 at commit 4245210, and in benchmark/alignment_benchmark.cpp):
  * speedup < 5% on all kernels     -> D-4 confirmed, close E-1, build no profile system
  * 5-20%                           -> D-4 stands; larger alignment documented as opt-in
  * > 20% on a per-frame kernel     -> reopen D-4, report before changing anything
"Speedup" means faster than the word-granularity baseline; a variant that is
SLOWER lands in the first band, and the size of the slowdown is reported rather
than clamped.

PLATFORM: the reference device -- AUTHORITATIVE. E-1 is a cache question, which
is exactly what a laptop hides (EXPERIMENTS.md "Measurement platforms").

  device:           pi4
  cpu:              Raspberry Pi 4 Model B Rev 1.5 (Cortex-A72)
  arch / kernel:    aarch64 / 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8
  compiler:         g++ (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG)
  governor:         performance (restored to ondemand on exit)
  core pinning:     taskset -c 3
  throttled before / after: throttled=0x0 / throttled=0x0  (every run)
  benchmark commit: 3383996

THREE RUNS, because one cannot show that a ratio reproduced. Ratios are on
medians of 9 interleaved batches; the spread column in each run's own output is
(max-min)/median and never exceeded 7%.

  640x480, uint32_t          run 1    run 2    run 3     footprint   AND fast path
  align 4 (word)  bitwiseAnd  1.000x   1.000x   1.000x     38400 B   contiguous
                  countNonZero 1.000x  1.000x   1.000x
  align 16        bitwiseAnd  1.008x   0.983x   1.001x     38400 B   contiguous
                  countNonZero 1.000x  1.000x   1.001x      (+0.0%)
  align 32        bitwiseAnd  0.321x   0.311x   0.321x     46080 B   row loop
                  countNonZero 0.999x  0.999x   0.998x     (+20.0%)
  align 64        bitwiseAnd  0.211x   0.205x   0.209x     61440 B   row loop
                  countNonZero 0.996x  0.996x   0.996x     (+60.0%)

  94x60 (pyr L3), uint32_t   run 1    run 2    run 3     footprint   AND fast path
  align 4 (word)  bitwiseAnd  1.000x   1.000x   1.000x       720 B   row loop
                  countNonZero 1.000x  1.000x   1.000x
  align 16        bitwiseAnd  0.988x   1.000x   0.993x       960 B   row loop
                  countNonZero 1.009x  1.000x   0.990x     (+33.3%)
  align 32        bitwiseAnd  0.991x   0.980x   0.994x      1920 B   row loop
                  countNonZero 0.974x  1.000x   0.999x    (+166.7%)
  align 64        bitwiseAnd  0.913x   0.874x   0.929x      3840 B   row loop
                  countNonZero 0.956x  0.947x   0.992x    (+433.3%)

READING. No alignment beats word granularity anywhere, on either kernel, in any
run. The best number in the whole table is 1.008x -- inside the 5-6% batch
spread of the row it sits in, so it is a null result and not a small win. The
rule's first band therefore applies, unambiguously: D-4 is confirmed, E-1 closes,
and no profile system gets built.

WHERE THE SLOWDOWNS COME FROM, since three of them are large. Two mechanisms,
both consequences of choosing the alignment rather than confounds to subtract:

  1. ops/logic.hpp's contiguous fast path requires every stride to equal the
     words a row needs. Over-aligning disables it, and bitwiseAnd at 640x480
     drops to 0.32x (align 32) and 0.21x (align 64) -- 3.1x and 4.8x SLOWER for
     20% and 60% more memory. At 94x60 the width is not a whole number of words,
     so no variant takes that path and no variant shows the cliff.
  2. Padding words are allocated and never read, so a wider stride spends cache
     lines and bandwidth on nothing. That is the residual 0.91-0.99x at 94x60,
     and it is why the align-64 row is the worst one there too.

countNonZero, which has no fast path and walks rows unconditionally, is flat to
within 0.5% at 640x480 across all four alignments. That is the clean isolation of
the alignment effect on its own: on this core it is zero.

WHAT ROW ALIGNMENT ACTUALLY DELIVERS. binMat allocates with new[], whose
guarantee here is 16 bytes, so rowAlignment aligns the row STRIDE and not the
base pointer -- the benchmark prints the measured base alignment per run and it
varies (16/32/64 B) with the allocator rather than with the requested alignment.
So this experiment shows that making rows mutually congruent buys nothing; it
does not test absolutely-64-byte-aligned rows, which no binCV API can currently
request. Nothing in the MVP asks for one.

X-7 CAVEAT. binCV builds with no -march flags, so the countNonZero rows measure
fmov/cnt/uaddlv/fmov per word as much as they measure alignment. That lowering is
identical across the four variants, so it cannot bias this comparison, but it
does mean the absolute ns/pixel figures move if a dispatch policy is ever chosen
(ROADMAP 2.3). No -march flag was added.

Full output of run 3 follows.

